  "query_preview": "..\n\nSprawling area of high pressure centered over the Dakotas is \nfunneling cold air southward into the Great Lakes behind last nights \ncold front. The combination of cold air advection lake clouds and \nwarm frontal clouds had lead to a very clouds day across the state. \nTemps have been slow to rise much into the 60s thus far so dropped \nforecast highs a few degrees. Lows tonight will fall into the 40s \nbut will be tempered by the blanket of clouds so chances of 30s are \nslim.  \n\nNext chance of rain comes Thursday as a pair of strong systems, \ncutoff low from the 4 corners region and a deepening trough dropping \nout of Canada, phase over the Midwest before passing through the \nGreat Lakes as an energetic positively tilted trough. Models are a \nlittle slower bringing the precip into SE MI, so pushed pops back \ntil after noon. The center of the surface low was supposed to pass \nnortheast through lower MI Thursday night, but as we get deeper into \nthe window of CAMs, they are suggesting that there will be a \nsecondary low farther east along the occluded front, staying just SE \nof MI, possible over Toledo. This doesn't do much for the expansive \narea of precipitation that will lift into the region along the warm \nconveyor belt, but it does bring convection into question, as this \nleading low looks to keep the instability gradient south of the \narea. CAPE values are coming in only around 100 J/kg or less in most \nmodel solutions. Will keep a mention of thunder in for now until we \ncan more clearly define the area of convection, or placement of the \nsurface warm front. QPF could range from a third to a half inch from \nThursday afternoon through Friday morning.  \n\nCold front will pass through early Friday pushing the brunt of the \nmoisture out of the area but cold advection will initiate lake \nprocesses introducing additional moisture to any lingering wrap \naround moisture around the exiting low. In addition, cold advection \nwill steepen lapse rates in the afternoon to around 10kft which will \nhelp scattered showers to develop. The deep mixing depths will also \nlead to a gusty day with winds solidly into the mid 20 knot range \nthrough the mixed layer throughout the day. Question is later in the \nevening as the upper level trough axis cross the area bringing a \nperiod of higher winds aloft into the 30-35 knot range. These winds \ncould be mixed down briefly before we attempt to decouple so could \nsee gusts increase around dusk for a few hours close to 40 mph. \n\nFriday night will be colder as thermal trough at 850mb dips down to \nbetween zero to -5C with skies clearing as high pressure builds back \nin. Friday highs only into the 50s will help lows drop into the 30s \nwith many locations across the northern CWA possibly seeing temps \nbelow freezing. Longwave ridge over the western conus and trough \nover the east will both be positively tilted heading through the \nweekend which will help delay the advancing thermal ridge back into \nthe region. Highs Saturday will hold int he 50s before beginning to \nmoderate Sunday back into the low 60s. Looks like ridging will hold \nover the region much of next week providing another lengthy stretch \nof dry weather.",
